  "result": "I wrote `test_generated_regression.py`. It passes on the before snapshot and fails on the after snapshot. The failure is an assertion on the Username label: `text-align` expected `'start'`, got `'center'`.\n\nThe test checks three things, with every expected value taken from the before snapshot:\n- The Username and Password labels have `text-align: start`.\n- The Sign Up button has `display: inline-block` with `0px` left and right margins.\n- The button's left edge is at x=56, give or take 1 px.\n\nThis test probably guards the wrong thing. The detector says the elements moved to right-aligned, but the snapshots and the diff show they went from left-aligned to centered. That matches the stated design intent (\"the Sign Up button ... was centered\"). The brief asked for a test that passes on before and fails on after, and it also said not to assert on intended changes. Both can't hold, so I followed the pass/fail requirement. As a result, the test pins the old left-aligned layout and would flag the intended centering as a regression. The detector's finding looks like a false positive.",
